rogrammatic Dependent Launch and Synchronization】\nL32:       * 【26†4.6. Green Contexts】\nL33:       * 【27†4.7. Locality Domains】\nL34:       * 【28†4.8. Lazy Loading】\nL35:       * 【29†4.9. Error Log Management】\nL36:       * 【30†4.10. Asynchronous Barriers】\nL37:       * 【31†4.11. Pipelines】\nL38:       * 【32†4.12. Asynchronous Data Copies】\nL39:       * 【33†4.13. Work Stealing with Cluster Launch Control】\nL40:       * 【34†4.14. L2 Cache Control】\nL41:       * 【35†4.15. Memory Synchronization Domains】\nL42:       * 【36†4.16. Interprocess Communication】\nL43:       * 【37†4.17. Virtual Memory Management】\nL44:       * 【38†4.18. Compute Fabric Transport】\nL45:       * 【39†4.19. Extended GPU Memory】\nL46:       * 【40†4.20. CUDA Dynamic Parallelism】\nL47:       * 【41†4.21. CUDA Interoperability with APIs】\nL48:       * 【42†4.22. Driver Entry Point Access】\nL49:     * 【43†5. Technical Appendices】\nL50:       * 【44†5.1. Compute Capabilities】\nL51:       * 【45†5.2. CUDA Environment Variables】\nL52:       * 【46†5.3. C++ Language Support】\nL53:       * 【47†5.4. C/C++ Language Extensions】\nL54:       * 【48†5.5. Floating-Point Computation】\nL55:       * 【49†5.6. Device-Callable APIs and Intrinsics】\nL56:       * 【50†5.7. CUDA C++ Memory Model】\nL57:       * 【51†5.8. CUDA C++ Execution model】\nL58:     * 【52†6. Notices】\nL59: # 4.2. CUDA Graphs【53†#】\nL60: CUDA Graphs present another model for work submission in CUDA. A graph is a series of operations such as kernel launches, data movement, etc., connected by dependencies. A graph is defined separately from its execution. This allows a graph to be defined once and then launched repeatedly.\nL61: Separating the definition of a graph from its execution enables a number of optimizations: first, CPU launch costs are reduced compared to streams, because much of the setup is done in advance; second, presenting the whole workflow to CUDA enables optimizations which might not be possible with the piecewise work submission mechanism of streams.\nL62: To see the optimizations possible with graphs, consider what happens in a stream: when you place a kernel into a stream, the host driver performs a sequence of operations in preparation for the execution of the kernel on the GPU. These operations, necessary for setting up and launching the kernel, are an overhead cost which must be paid for each kernel that is issued. For a GPU kernel with a short execution time, this overhead cost can be a significant fraction of the overall end-to-end execution time.\nL63: By creating a CUDA graph that encompasses a workflow that will be launched many times, these overhead costs can be paid once for the entire graph during instantiation, and the graph itself can then be launched repeatedly with very little overhead.\nL64: ## 4.2.1. Graph Structure【54†#】\nL65: \nL66: An operation forms a node in a graph. The dependencies between the operations are the edges. These dependencies constrain the execution sequence of the operations.\nL67: \nL68: An operation may be scheduled at any time once the nodes on which it depends are complete. Scheduling is left up to the CUDA system.\nL69: ### 4.2.1.1. Node Types【55†#】\nL70: \nL71: A graph node can be one of:\nL72: \nL73:   * kernel\nL74: \nL75:   * CPU function call\nL76: \nL77:   * memory copy\nL78: \nL79:   * memset\nL80: \nL81:   * empty node\nL82: \nL83:   * waiting on a 【56†CUDA Event】\nL84: \nL85:   * recording a 【56†CUDA Event】\nL86: \nL87:   * signalling an 【57†external semaphore】\nL88: \nL89:   * waiting on an 【57†external semaphore】\nL90: \nL91:   * 【58†conditional node】\nL92: \nL93:   * 【59†memory node】\nL94: \nL95:   * child graph: To execute a separate nested graph, as shown in the following figure.\nL96: \nL97: Figure 24 Child Graph Example【60†#】\nL98: ### 4.2.1.2. Edge Data【61†#】\nL99: \nL100: CUDA 12.3 introduced edge data on CUDA Graphs. At this time, the only use for non-default edge data is enabling 【62†Programmatic Dependent Launch】.\nL101: Generally speaking, edge data modifies a dependency specified by an edge and consists of three parts: an outgoing port, an incoming port, and a type. An outgoing port specifies when an associated edge is triggered. An incoming port specifies what portion of a node is dependent on an associated edge. A type modifies the relation between the endpoints.\nL102: Port values are specific to node type and direction, and edge types may be restricted to specific node types. In all cases, zero-initialized edge data represents default behavior. Outgoing port 0 waits on an entire task, incoming port 0 blocks an entire task, and edge type 0 is associated with a full dependency with memory synchronizing behavior.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn2view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/employing-cuda-graphs-in-a-dynamic-environment/\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/employing-cuda-graphs-in-a-dynamic-environment/: (400) Timeout fetching--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn2view3 [wordlim: 200] Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view1\",\"id\":96}); \nFailed to fetch https://github.com/THU-HAS/MegaZK/blob/main/LICENSE-MIT: Cache miss--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn2view4 [wordlim: 200] Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view1\",\"id\":94}); \nFailed to fetch https://github.com/THU-HAS/MegaZK/blob/main/LICENSE: Cache miss"
